sections.s2_technical:
Three requirements make a good key. First, override equals so two objects representing the same logical key compare as equal. Second, override hashCode from the same fields so equal keys produce equal hashCodes and are routed to the same bucket. Third, make the key immutable, at least for the fields that equals and hashCode use, so the key's hashCode is stable for its whole life in the map.

Here is the correct key class in finance terms, using a composite key of branch code and account number:

```java
public final class AccountKey {
    private final String branchCode;   // immutable
    private final String accountNumber; // immutable
        public AccountKey(String b, String a) { this.branchCode = b;
        this.accountNumber = a; }
    @Override public boolean equals(Object o) {
        if (this == o) return true;
        if (!(o instanceof AccountKey)) return false;
        AccountKey k = (AccountKey) o;
        return java.util.Objects.equals(branchCode, k.branchCode)
            && java.util.Objects.equals(accountNumber, k.accountNumber);
    }
    @Override public int hashCode() { return java.util.Objects.hash(branchCode, accountNumber); }
}
```

Now `map.put(new AccountKey("MUM", "SAV2001"), tier)` and a later `map.get(new AccountKey("MUM", "SAV2001"))` work, because the two keys are equal and share a bucket.

Immutability matters because of a specific failure. If a key is mutable and you change a field that hashCode depends on after inserting it, the stored entry stays in the bucket chosen by the old hashCode, but any lookup now computes the new hashCode and searches a different bucket, so the entry becomes unreachable, a leak you cannot get or remove. Making the key final with final fields and no setters prevents this entirely.

A senior point is to prefer a dedicated immutable key type over using a mutable domain entity as a key. A domain Account object often has a mutable balance and status, and even if those are not in equals, using it as a key invites accidents, so a small immutable AccountKey is safer and clearer. Another senior point is that records in modern Java are perfect keys because they are immutable and generate correct equals and hashCode automatically, for example `record AccountKey(String branchCode, String accountNumber) {}`.

